# SPDX-License-Identifier: CERN-OHL-P-2.0
#
# Colorlight i9 v7.2 — one oca_core behind one Ethernet port.
# LFE5U-45F-6BG381C, CABGA381, speed grade 6 (BOM-MVP.md).
#
# PROVENANCE OF EVERY BALL. Each one was read out of prjtrellis's own IO
# database for this device and package —
# tools/trellis/share/trellis/database/ECP5/LFE5U-45F/iodb.json, package
# CABGA381, whose 381 balls carry 203 user IO pads in that database —
# for existence, bank and clock capability, then
# cross-checked against two independent pin lists: litex-boards'
# colorlight_i5.py (_io_v7_2, BSD-2-Clause) and the board vendor's own
# colorlight_i9_v7.2.md. All three agree on all seventeen pins below.
#
# The vendor numbers the two PHYs the other way round from litex: the
# bank 6 port constrained here is litex's eth0 and the vendor's
# ETH-PHY1 (U30). litex says so itself, in a comment — the numbering
# follows the PHYA[0] straps, not the silkscreen. Which RJ45 that is on
# the board is a bench question, not a file question.
#
# ONE BANK DECIDES EVERYTHING. Every pin in this file is in bank 6: the
# 25 MHz oscillator (P3), the LED (L2), the PHY reset (P4), MDIO and MDC
# (P5, N5), and all twelve RGMII signals. The design document records
# that Ethernet port 0 and the LED share bank 6; the clock, the reset
# and the management pins are in it too, so this build has exactly one
# VCCIO to get right and no second bank to hide a mistake in.
#
# And this file cannot set it. BANK and BANK_VCC are accepted by
# nextpnr's LPF parser and stored on the cell (ecp5/lpf.cc:38) and then
# never read by anything — a grep over the whole ecp5 backend finds that
# one line and nothing else. The single exception is bank 8, the
# configuration bank, whose VCCIO comes from SYSCONFIG CONFIG_IOVOLTAGE
# (ecp5/bitstream.cc:684). Bank 6's VCCIO is a board rail. It is
# measured, never declared.
#
# MEASURED 2026-08-11: 3.28 V, which is the 3.3 V rail. oca_vccio drove
# eight free bank 6 balls high and low in step with D2; two of them
# surface on the carrier's P4, at pins 8 and 25, and both read 3.28 V
# driven high and 0 V driven low. An output cannot rise above its own
# bank rail, so a pad reading 3.28 V is a bank at 3.3 V within the
# meter's accuracy, and 2.5 V is not a candidate at that distance. The
# hard 0 V is what rules out an undriven pin reading a neighbour.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# IO_TYPE: LVCMOS33, and what would follow from that being wrong
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two sources, and they contradict each other. litex-boards drives every
# clock, LED and Ethernet signal on this board family as LVCMOS33 —
# checked line by line in colorlight_i5.py, all of clk25, user_led_n,
# eth_clocks 0/1 and eth 0/1, on both the 7.0 map and the 7.2 deep copy.
# The vendor's own src/i9/blink/blink.lpf declares the LED LVCMOS25.
#
# But read the vendor's file whole, because it is six lines long and it
# contradicts itself:
#
#     LOCATE COMP "clk_i" SITE "P3";
#     IOBUF PORT "clk_i" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;
#     FREQUENCY PORT "clk_i" 25 MHZ;
#     LOCATE COMP "led_o" SITE "L2";
#     IOBUF PORT "led_o" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS25;
#
# P3 and L2 are both in bank 6. The vendor declares one of them 3V3 and
# the other 2V5 in the same bank of the same file, and nextpnr builds it
# without a murmur — because its bank consistency check runs only over
# outputs and referenced types (ecp5/bitstream.cc:653), so the input at
# P3 neither votes nor is checked, and the LED is the only voter there
# is. The vendor's LVCMOS25 is therefore not evidence about the rail: it
# is the one line in that file that nothing could have checked.
#
# So LVCMOS33 is taken here, on four grounds. Both sources agree on P3,
# in bank 6, and only the LED is in dispute. litex's declaration covers
# all seventeen pins and is what LiteEth designs on this board family
# are built with, while the vendor's covers two. DRIVE can only be
# written for LVCMOS33 (ecp5/bitstream.cc:888; anything else warns once
# and drops the attribute), so choosing 2V5 would forfeit the one
# constraint-file knob on transmit edge rate — the knob the design
# document's falsifiable prediction about the transmit direction may
# need. And a wrong declaration here damages nothing: the driver is
# powered from VCCIO whatever it is called, so the FPGA cannot be made
# to drive above the rail by a line of text.
#
# What each wrong combination actually does, since "it is wrong" is not
# a symptom anyone can look for:
#
#  * Bank 6 is 3V3, declared LVCMOS33. THIS IS THE MEASURED CASE, and
#    it is correct. The four below are kept because a later revision or
#    a different board makes them live again, and because "it is wrong"
#    is not a symptom anyone can look for.
#
#  * Bank 6 is 2V5, declared LVCMOS33 — OUTPUTS. Builds, programs, runs.
#    BASE_TYPE becomes OUTPUT_LVCMOS33 (ecp5/bitstream.cc:832) and the
#    drive-strength codes are the 3V3 ladder applied to a 2V5 rail, so
#    edge rate and output impedance are not what the codes say. TXD and
#    TXC still swing 0..2.5 V, which the B50612D accepts on a 2V5
#    OVDD_RGMII. Most likely works; margin unquantified.
#
#  * Bank 6 is 2V5, declared LVCMOS33 — INPUTS. THE SILENT CASE. A pure
#    input never enters the bank consistency check at all — the
#    condition is `dir != "INPUT" || is_referenced(...)`, and LVCMOS33
#    is not a referenced standard — so the seven receive pins here vote
#    on nothing and are checked against nothing. But their IO_TYPE is
#    still written: BASE_TYPE becomes INPUT_LVCMOS33 unconditionally,
#    which moves the receiver threshold to roughly half of 3.3 V against
#    a signal that only reaches 2.5 V. High-side noise margin drops from
#    about 1.25 V to about 0.85 V on five DDR lines at 125 MHz. There is
#    no diagnostic anywhere in the flow. This is the combination that
#    produces a link which comes up and drops packets, and it is why the
#    rail was measured before the RGMII pins were ever driven rather
#    than after the first symptom. It is not the case this board is in.
#
#  * Bank 6 is 3V3, declared LVCMOS25. Builds. Receiver threshold sits
#    near 1.25 V against a 3.3 V swing, which is generous on the high
#    side and thin on the low side, and DRIVE is silently unavailable.
#
# The blink test of bring-up step 2 cannot resolve any of this, which is
# why oca_vccio exists. On the i5 the LED was U16, in bank 3; the v7.2
# deep copy moved it to L2, in bank 6. A blink design contains one
# output, so that output alone fixes bank 6's VCCIO in the bitstream and
# the LED lights on either rail. What settles the rail is reading a
# driven pad with a meter, which is what oca_vccio is for.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW THIS FILE CAN BE WRONG WITHOUT SAYING SO, AND WHAT CATCHES IT
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# A LOCATE COMP or IOBUF PORT naming a cell that does not exist is
# skipped with no message at all (ecp5/lpf.cc:144 and :155 — the whole
# body is inside `if (fnd_cell != cells.end())`). Rename a port in the
# top and its constraint here stops applying, silently.
#
# Three things do catch it, and they are the reason this file is
# checkable at all:
#
#  1. Any top-level IO with no LOCATE is a hard error naming the port —
#     "IO '<name>' is unconstrained in LPF" (ecp5/main.cc:287).
#     run_synth.py deliberately does not pass --lpf-allow-unconstrained.
#  2. Therefore, COUNTING IS A PROOF. This file constrains 17 ports. If
#     the design also has 17 top-level IO and the build does not raise
#     the unconstrained error, then no LOCATE here is stale: a stale one
#     would leave one real port unconstrained. Both halves are needed;
#     neither alone says anything.
#  3. A FREQUENCY line naming a net that does not exist DOES warn —
#     "net '<name>' does not exist in design, ignoring clock
#     constraint" (common/kernel/basectx.cc:108). The two FREQUENCY
#     lines below therefore double as a spelling check on clk25 and
#     rgmii_rx_clk.
#
# The top-level port list this file requires, exactly:
#
#     clk25, led_n, phy_rst_n, phy_mdio, phy_mdc,
#     rgmii_rx_clk, rgmii_rxd[3:0], rgmii_rx_ctl,
#     rgmii_tx_clk, rgmii_txd[3:0], rgmii_tx_ctl
#
# The RGMII names are oca_rgmii.sv's own pad-side port names, so the top
# can pass them straight through. Vector bits are constrained per bit
# because that is how nextpnr names them; a one-bit vector would also be
# matched by its scalar name (ecp5/lpf.cc:139).
#
# The two vectors must be declared [3:0] and not [0:3]. nextpnr builds a
# buffer name per port bit as base + '[' + (offset + index) + ']' for a
# downto port and offset + length - index - 1 for an upto one
# (frontend/frontend_base.h:367-381). Both declarations produce the same
# four names, wired to opposite bits. Nothing would complain and the
# nibbles would be reversed on the wire.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Ethernet port 1 (bank 3: U19 U20 T19 T20 R20 P19 L19 P20 N19 N20 M19
# M20) is not here. The area measurement says two ports do not fit —
# 94.5% of the device with two cores, and this device stopped routing at
# 76.4%. Adding those twelve lines would also put a second, independent
# VCCIO in play.
#
# The reset button at K18 is not here either: it is in bank 2, and this
# build takes its reset from the PLL lock (oca_clkrst.sv). Adding it
# means adding a bank.
#
# No SYSCONFIG. Nothing in this build boots from flash — bring-up loads
# over JTAG — so ecppack's defaults stand, and the only VCCIO SYSCONFIG
# could set is bank 8's.
#
# No BLOCK RESETPATHS or BLOCK ASYNCPATHS. LiteX emits both into every
# .lpf it writes and nextpnr accepts both and then does nothing with
# them (ecp5/lpf.cc:86-89 has no action, only a warning for the forms it
# does not know). They would be cargo.
#
# No DRIVE, PULLMODE or SLEWRATE. litex sets none of them on these
# seventeen pins either, so the ecppack defaults are what this board
# family is known to run with. DRIVE remains available on every pin
# here, which is a consequence of the LVCMOS33 choice above.
#
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLOCKS
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Only two clocks enter the chip through a pin, and only those two are
# declared here. The other two — clk_sys and clk_tx — come out of the
# PLL, and nextpnr DERIVES their constraints from the EHXPLLL
# parameters: it reads the constraint on CLKI, multiplies by
# CLKFB_DIV * CLKOP_DIV / CLKI_DIV to get the VCO, divides by each
# output divider, and logs "Derived frequency constraint of X MHz for
# net Y" (ecp5/pack.cc:2982-3032). Constraining them here as well would
# need their post-synthesis hierarchical net names, which this file
# cannot know, and would only be able to disagree with the derivation.
#
# So the check on the PLL is to read those two log lines. With
# oca_clkrst.sv's parameters they must say 125.0 MHz and 48.1 MHz. Note
# that nextpnr reports a VCO outside the legal 400-800 MHz range with
# log_info and nothing stronger (ecp5/pack.cc:3006), which is why
# oca_clkrst.sv carries its own elaboration guard on it.
#
# rgmii_rx_clk is constrained at its gigabit rate. The PHY drives it at
# 25 MHz at 100BASE-TX and 2.5 MHz at 10BASE-T, so 125 MHz is the worst
# case and the one worth constraining — but note that it is not present
# at all until the link is up.

FREQUENCY PORT "clk25" 25 MHZ;
FREQUENCY PORT "rgmii_rx_clk" 125 MHZ;

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bank 6 — oscillator, LED, PHY management
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# P3 is LLC_GPLL0T_IN, a dedicated PLL reference input: the twelve such
# balls on CABGA381 are A4/A5, A6/B6, A19/B20, C18/D17, P3/P4 and
# U16/T17, and P3 is one of them. That is what lets oca_clkrst.sv drive
# EHXPLLL.CLKI without general routing.
#
# P4, the PHY reset, is LLC_GPLL0C_IN — the complement half of P3's own
# pair. It is an ordinary PIO and driving it as an output is fine, but
# it does mean the differential form of this PLL input is spoken for.
#
# L2 is the LED, D2 on the silkscreen. Active low, from litex's
# user_led_n and confirmed on the board 2026-08-11 by oca_blink's
# asymmetric duty cycle. It moved here from U16 in the v7.2 revision,
# which is what put it in the same bank as Ethernet port 0.

LOCATE COMP "clk25"     SITE "P3";
IOBUF  PORT "clk25"     IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "led_n"     SITE "L2";
IOBUF  PORT "led_n"     IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "phy_rst_n" SITE "P4";
IOBUF  PORT "phy_rst_n" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "phy_mdio"  SITE "P5";
IOBUF  PORT "phy_mdio"  IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "phy_mdc"   SITE "N5";
IOBUF  PORT "phy_mdc"   IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bank 6 — RGMII, receive
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# H2 is PCLKT6_0, a primary clock input for this half of the bank, which
# is what lets the recovered clock reach a global without going through
# fabric. No PLL can clean it up: the dedicated PLL reference inputs are
# the twelve balls listed above and H2 is not among them.
#
# All five receive pins are on the left edge (column 0, rows 35-68), so
# each has a full IOLOGIC rather than the reduced SIOLOGIC that top and
# bottom IO get (nextpnr chooses by row, ecp5/pack.cc:1988) — DELAYF and
# IDDRX1F both need it. They are also why these LOCATEs are load
# bearing: nextpnr refuses to absorb a delay or a DDR register into a
# PIO that is not pin-constrained, with "IOLOGIC functionality (DDR,
# DELAY, DQS, etc) can only be used with pin-constrained PIO"
# (ecp5/pack.cc:1975). Delete one of these five lines and the build
# stops with that message rather than placing the cell somewhere else.

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_rx_clk" SITE "H2";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_rx_clk" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_rx_ctl" SITE "P2";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_rx_ctl" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_rxd[0]" SITE "K2";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_rxd[0]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_rxd[1]" SITE "L1";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_rxd[1]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_rxd[2]" SITE "N1";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_rxd[2]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_rxd[3]" SITE "P1";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_rxd[3]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Bank 6 — RGMII, transmit
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# G1, which the vendor's table calls GTXCLK, is PCLKC6_0 — the
# complement of the very pair whose true half, H2, carries the receive
# clock. Two 125 MHz clocks on one differential pair of balls, one in
# and one out. Nothing in the flow objects and nothing about it is our
# choice, but it is worth knowing before blaming the delay value for
# marginal receive.
#
# G2 is PCLKT6_1 and J3 is GR_PCLK6_1: clock-capable balls carrying
# plain data here, which costs nothing but does spend two of the bank's
# clock inputs.

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_tx_clk" SITE "G1";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_tx_clk" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_tx_ctl" SITE "K1";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_tx_ctl" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_txd[0]" SITE "G2";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_txd[0]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_txd[1]" SITE "H1";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_txd[1]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_txd[2]" SITE "J1";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_txd[2]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;

LOCATE COMP "rgmii_txd[3]" SITE "J3";
IOBUF  PORT "rgmii_txd[3]" IO_TYPE=LVCMOS33;
